<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6680"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beck<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beck<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 09, 2005 - 6:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I was just on KAKE.com, and I saw the second part of the Relford interview. Here&#39;s the link to it... <BR> <BR><a href="http://ww2.kake.com/global/video/popup/pop_index.asp?ClipID1=346007&amp;h1=BTK%20Back%3A%20A%20Mother%27s%20Murder&amp;vt1=v&amp;at1=News&amp;d1=237600&amp;LaunchPageAdTag=Homepage&amp;activePane=info&amp;playerVersion=9&amp;rnd=41055457" target=_top>http://ww2.kake.com/global/video/popup/pop_index.a sp?ClipID1=346007&amp;h1=BTK%20Back%3A%20A%20Mother%27 s%20Murder&amp;vt1=v&amp;at1=News&amp;d1=237600&amp;LaunchPageAdTa g=Homepage&amp;activePane=info&amp;playerVersion=9&amp;rnd=410 55457</a> <BR> <BR>I have very slow dial-up, and when they talk about when BTK wrote about a phone call saved them, Steven is holding up a yellow piece of paper... is that supposed to be BTK&#39;s handwriting?  <BR> <BR>Another thing, there&#39;s another thread about Shirley Vian not being the intended victim, another woman was. Also, before this interview took place, I think it was a common thought that the picture was not of Vian at all. In this part of the interview, Steven remembers the picture being Shirley and him. Do you think he&#39;s remembering this correctly, or if it&#39;s a jumbled memory? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6680-->
<!--Post: 6691--><!--Time: 1107963367-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6691"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lazysean<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lazysean<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 09, 2005 - 7:36 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->No, I don&#39;t think that is supposed to be BTK&#39;s handwriting.  For one thing I think all of his letters were typed.  You can also see a heading at the top of the paper, whoever wrote it labelled it as something like &#34;from pg 2 of btk letter...&#34; &#40;sorry i don&#39;t have the picture in front of me&#41;.  Anyway, it looks like it&#39;s just an excerpt of the letter that someone copied down to show Stephen, not the original &#40;or even complete&#41; letter itself. <BR> <BR>As for whether Stephen is accurately remembering the photo -- it&#39;s hard to say.  There are a lot of things I wouldn&#39;t trust his memory for -- a good description of a man he saw 30 years ago for less than 40 minutes is definitely one of them.  Exactly what the man was wearing, what type of gun or holster it was, etc.  His memories on those type of things are certainly no longer pure, original memories, but &#34;memories of memories&#34; as someone else put it. <BR> <BR>The photograph, though, seems different to me.  For one thing, it doesn&#39;t seem like he was shown the photo on purpose, ie by someone trying to jar his memory and get him to identify it.  He just saw it one day at his grandmother&#39;s house and instantly thought &#34;Oh my god!  That&#39;s the same photo that guy showed me!&#34;   Maybe I&#39;m inferring too much, just something about the scenario really rings true for me, and I believe him on that part.  It also helps that he supposedly ID&#39;d the picture just a few years afterwards, rather than 30. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 6691-->
